      out: Data engineering involves developing, implementing, and maintaining systems and processes that handle raw data, transforming it into high-quality, consistent information for use by data scientists, analysts, and other stakeholders [Ch. 1]. It encompasses the movement, manipulation, and management of data [Ch. 1], and is the intersection of security, data management, DataOps, data architecture, orchestration, and software engineering [Ch. 1]. The core purpose is to design robust and reliable systems to carry data through its lifecycle and serve it according to end-user needs [Ch. 4]. Data engineers ensure data remains available and usable, setting up and operating the organization’s data infrastructure [Ch. 1].
  --- rewrite (0.26s)
      in : Sentence: When we unpack the common threads of how various people define data engineering, an obvious pattern emerges: a data engineer gets data, stores it, and prepares it for consumption by data scientists, analysts, and others.
      out: Data engineers get data, store it, and prepare it for others.
  --- rewrite (0.2s)
      in : Sentence: Data engineering is all about the movement, manipulation, and management of data.
      out: Data engineering handles moving, changing, and managing data.
  --- rewrite (0.29s)
      in : Sentence: However, it’s easy to get caught up in chasing bleeding-edge technology while losing sight of the core purpose of data engineering: designing robust and reliable systems to carry data through the full lifecycle and serve it according to the needs of end users.
      out: It's easy to focus on new tech and forget the main goal of data engineering.
  --- rewrite (0.18s)
      in : Sentence: It takes dedicated specialists—data engineers—to maintain data so that it remains available and usable by others.
      out: Data engineers keep data available for others to use.
